course: dictionary
lesson: 10240
title: A word from French
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some English words come from other languages. Toilette comes from French. It is a formal, old-fashioned word, so context and the French clues help you work out what it means. In old novels, a rich lady spends time at her toilette while her maids help her, so the word is about getting ready.
explain: The same word can have more than one meaning. In French, «Je peux aller aux toilettes, s'il vous plaît ?» means "May I go to the toilet, please?" Notice the s on toilettes: in French it is plural, and it means the toilets or washroom.
explain: When a word has several meanings, try each one in the sentence and see which fits. «She spent an hour at her toilette.» Getting ready fits, so that is the meaning here.
fact: Toilette is a noun, and in English it is uncountable: you do not say "a toilette".
fact: Toilette can mean the process of washing, dressing and doing your hair.
fact: In French, toilettes (with an s) means toilets or washroom, and it is plural.
fact: Toilette can also mean a woman's elegant outfit, but this use is old-fashioned.
fact: The English word toilet comes from this French word, and toilette sounds like twah-LET.
exercise: In old novels, a rich lady spent an hour at her toilette. What does toilette mean here? | A washroom in a French school | A small wooden table | A cold glass of lemonade | * Washing, dressing and doing her hair
exercise: Où sont les toilettes? What does this French question mean in English? | * Where are the toilets? | What time is it? | Where is my bag? | Where is the train station?
exercise: Which language does the word toilette come from? | * French | German | Latin | Spanish
test: «Je peux aller aux toilettes, s'il vous plaît ?» Which French word means toilets? | aller | toilette | * toilettes | peux
test: In an old story, a lady dressed in a beautiful toilette for the party. What does toilette mean here? | A cold drink | A washroom | * Her elegant outfit | A type of French bread
exam: Liam says toilette always means the loo. What is the best answer? | It means a bed | Yes, always, in every language | * No, in English it can also mean getting ready, and in French toilettes means toilets
